¶
'''bestial''' (bes′tịal), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''bestialis'', < ''bestia'', beast: see ''beast.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Belonging to a beast or to the class of beasts; animal.
{{smaller block|Of shape part human, part ''bestial.''{{right|''Tatler'', No. 49.}}}}
'''2.''' Having the qualities of a beast; brutal; below the dignity of reason or humanity; carnal: as, a ''bestial'' appetite.
{{smaller block|I have lost the immortal part of myself, and what remains is ''bestial.''{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Bestial automaton.''' See ''automaton.''—'''Bestial sign,''' in ''astrol.'', a zodiacal sign denoted by a quadruped, Aries, Taurus, Leo, Sagittarius, or Capricornus.='''Syn.''' ''Brutish'', ''Bestial'', etc. (see ''brute''); vile, depraved, sensual.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' [< LL. ''bestiale'', cattle, neut. of L. ''bestialis:'' see above.] '''1.''' In ''Scots law'', the cattle on a farm taken collectively.—'''2†.''' A work on zoölogy. ''Brewer.''

'''bestiality''' (bes-tịal′i-ti), ''n.'' [< LL. ''bestialitas'', < ''bestialis:'' see ''bestial.''] '''1.''' The qualities or nature of a beast; conduct or mental condition unworthy of human nature; beastliness.
{{smaller block|What can be a greater absurdity than to affirm ''bestiality'' to be the essence of humanity, and darkness the centre of light?{{right|''Martinus Scriblerus.''}}}}
'''2.''' Unnatural connection with a beast.

'''bestialize''' (bes′tial-īz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''bestialized'', ppr. ''bestializing''. [< ''bestial'', ''a.'', + ''-ize.''] To make like a beast; bring or reduce to the state or condition of a beast.

