{{smaller block|''Bestuck'' with slanderous darts.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 536.}}}}

{{smaller block|In these little visual interpretations [valentines] no emblem is so common as the heart, . . . the ''bestuck'' and bleeding heart. {{right|''Lamb'', Valentine’s Day.}}}}

'''bestill''' (bē̤-stil′), ''v. t.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''still''<sup>1</sup>.] To make quiet or still.

{{smaller block|Commerce ''bestilled'' her many-nationed tongue.
{{right|''J. Cunningham'', Elegiac Ode.}}}}

{{smaller block|[In the following passage uncertain:
<poem>They, ''bestill’d''
Almost to jelly with the act of fear,
Stand dumb, and speak not to him.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 2.}}
This is the reading of the folios; the quartos and modern editions read ''distilled''.]}}

'''bestir''' (bē̤-stėr′), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''bestirred'', ppr. ''bestirring''. [< ME. ''bestyrien'', ''bestirien'', ''besterien'', bestir, < AS. ''bestyrian'', heap up, pile up, < ''be-'' + ''styrian'', stir: see ''be-''<sup>1</sup> and ''stir''.] To put into brisk or vigorous action; reflexively, move with life and vigor: as, ''bestir yourself''.

{{smaller block|You have . . . ''bestirred'' your valour.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, ii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Come on, clowns, forsake your dumps,
And ''bestir'' your hobnailed stumps.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', The Satyr.}}}}

{{smaller block|Rouse and ''bestir themselves'' ere well awake.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 334.}}}}

'''bestness''' (best′nes), ''n.'' [< ''best'' + ''-ness''.] The quality of being best. [Rare.]

